{"name":"Grey Squirrel","hex":"#989081","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grey squirrel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["UK standard spelling of gray squirrel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey_squirrel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gray squirrel","blocks":[{"text":"Gray squirrel or grey squirrel may refer to several species of squirrel indigenous to North America:"},{"items":["The eastern gray squirrel, from the eastern United States and southeastern Canada; introduced into Great Britain, Ireland, western North America, Italy, and South Africa","The western gray squirrel, from the western United States","The Arizona gray squirrel, from the southwestern United States and adjacent Mexico","The Mexican gray squirrel, from southern Mexico and Guatemala; introduced into the Florida Keys"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_squirrel","wikidata":"Q26261840"},{"title":"Squirrel","blocks":[{"text":"Squirrels are members of the family Sciuridae, a family that includes small or medium-sized rodents. The squirrel family includes tree squirrels, ground squirrels, and flying squirrels. Squirrels are indigenous to the Americas, Eurasia, and Africa, and were introduced by humans to Australia. The earliest known fossilized squirrels date from the Eocene epoch, and among other living rodent families, the squirrels are most closely related to the mountain beaver and dormice."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Sciuridae.jpg/330px-Sciuridae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squirrel","wikidata":"Q9482"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Sciuridae_distribution.png/500px-Sciuridae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Sciuridae_distribution.png/1280px-Sciuridae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Sciuridae_distribution.png/500px-Sciuridae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASciuridae_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Sciurus_carolinensis_-British_Columbia%2C_Canada-8.jpg/500px-Sciurus_carolinensis_-British_Columbia%2C_Canada-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Sciurus_carolinensis_-British_Columbia%2C_Canada-8.jpg/500px-Sciurus_carolinensis_-British_Columbia%2C_Canada-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reaching out for food on a garden bird feeder, this squirrel can rotate its hind feet, allowing it to descend a tree headfirst.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASciurus_carolinensis_-British_Columbia%2C_Canada-8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Ratufa_skull.JPG/500px-Ratufa_skull.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Ratufa_skull.JPG/1280px-Ratufa_skull.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb